{0 Streams}

A {!Workero.Stream.t} is a JavaScript [ReadableStream] of bytes. Use it to read
a large body in chunks, to send a body that you build in steps, and to pass a
body from one server to another without a copy.

{1 Read a request body in chunks}

{!Workero.Request.body} gives the body as a stream. It is [None] when the
request has no body, for example for [GET].

{@ocaml[
let handler req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let total = ref 0 in
  Option.iter
    (Stream.iter (fun chunk -> total := !total + String.length chunk))
    (Request.body req);
  Response.text (Printf.sprintf "%d bytes\n" !total)

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

{!Workero.Stream.iter} calls your function on each chunk. It waits for the next
chunk like {!Workero.Promise.await}. Process chunks as they arrive instead of
collecting the whole body in memory.

For more control, lock the stream with {!Workero.Stream.reader}.
{!Workero.Stream.Reader.read} gives [Some chunk], or [None] at the end.
{!Workero.Stream.Reader.cancel} tells the source to stop. A stream has one
reader: a second {!Workero.Stream.reader} raises {!Workero.Js_error}.

Getting a handle with {!Workero.Request.body} does not read or lock the body.
The body is consumed when you read its stream or call a body reader such as
{!Workero.Request.text}, {!Workero.Request.bytes} or
{!Workero.Request.form_data}. Do not use two readers on the same body. To read
it twice, call {!Workero.Request.clone} before consuming either copy.

{1 Send a body in steps}

{!Workero.Response.stream} returns a response with a streaming body. Your
function starts immediately in its own fiber and writes the body. Synchronous
work before its first suspension can delay the response.

{@ocaml[
let handler _req _env ctx =
  let open Workero in
  Response.stream ctx (fun writer ->
      for i = 1 to 5 do
        Stream.Writer.write writer (Printf.sprintf "chunk %d\n" i);
        Promise.await (Timer.sleep 1000)
      done)

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

- The body ends when your function returns.
- If your function raises, the body ends with an error. The error goes to
  [console.error].
- [status] and [headers] work as in {!Workero.Response.text}.
- The function runs through {!Workero.Context.wait_until}. While the client
  remains connected and receives the stream, the HTTP invocation stays active.
  After the response ends or the client disconnects, remaining [waitUntil]
  work has a shared limit of 30 seconds. See {{!page-async}Async and background work}.

To send a body and read another one at the same time, use
{!Workero.Stream.pipe}. It gives a {!Workero.Stream.Writer.t} and the
{!Workero.Stream.t} that reads what you write.

{1 Pass a body through}

{!Workero.Response.of_stream} sends a stream as the body without buffering it.
It creates a new response with status 200 and the headers you pass. If you
want to forward an upstream response without changing its status or headers,
use {!Workero.Response.copy} instead:

{@ocaml[
let handler _req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let upstream = fetch "https://example.com/big-file" in
  Response.copy upstream

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

To change only headers or status, pass [~headers] or [~status] to
{!Workero.Response.copy}. It preserves the upstream status and headers when
you omit those arguments. If you already have a {!Workero.Stream.t}, use
{!Workero.Response.of_stream} with the intended status and headers. See
{{!page-requests}Requests and responses}.

{!Workero.Response.of_stream} and {!Workero.Response.copy} pass the existing
body stream to the new response; they do not read it when they get the handle.
The original and new responses then share a body that cannot be consumed twice.
Call {!Workero.Response.clone} before either copy consumes the body if you
need to read both.
To read both, call {!Workero.Response.clone} before consuming either body.

{1 Back-pressure}

{!Workero.Stream.Writer.write} waits until the reader can take more data. A
slow client slows your loop. The Worker does not fill its memory with chunks
that nobody has read.

{1 Client disconnect}

The Worker gets no disconnect event when the client leaves. A later
{!Workero.Stream.Writer.write} can raise {!Workero.Js_error}. Catch it to
clean up.

{@ocaml[
let handler _req _env ctx =
  let open Workero in
  Response.stream ctx (fun writer ->
      try
        while true do
          Stream.Writer.write writer "tick\n";
          Promise.await (Timer.sleep 1000)
        done
      with Js_error _ -> ())

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

A loop that does not write cannot see the disconnect.

{1 Close and abort}

{!Workero.Stream.Writer.close} ends the stream. {!Workero.Stream.Writer.abort}
ends it with an error, and the client sees a failed body.
{!Workero.Response.stream} closes the writer for you when your function
returns, and aborts it when your function raises.
